    "weather": [
      "January is slush to the ankle at every crosswalk, and the church vestibule is a lake by the second Mass.",
      "Spring is two good weeks in late April that the whole city treats as a personal favor.",
      "August smells of garbage and ocean, and the churches without air conditioning empty out by the second reading.",
      "October upstate is the Hudson gone gold under the Catskills; October in the Bronx is the same wind with nothing to move."
    ],
    "sunday": [
      "Sunday on Park Avenue is a full church and an empty school; Sunday in the Bronx is the reverse, and louder.",
      "The Spanish Mass runs long and the English Mass runs short, and the street parking is a penance no confessor assigned.",
      "The tourists at the cathedral photograph the consecration, and the ushers have given up on stopping them."
    ],
    "presbyterate": [
      "The priests of New York are old, sharp, and fond of each other in the way of men who have shared a long siege.",
      "They rank each other by seminary class, by parish, and by which archbishop made them, and they are rarely wrong.",
      "A thing said at a clergy dinner on Thursday is known at the chancery by Friday and in the tabloid by Sunday."
    ]
  },
